 ### Noise Floor Estimation via MAD
-Noise Floor Estimation via MAD estimates the noise standard deviation $\sigma$ using Median Absolute Deviation (MAD) estimators to determine the theoretical maximum PSNR (noise floor) for a volume [S7, unofficial].
-- **Laplacian MAD**: Applies the D-dimensional discrete Laplacian and scales the MAD of the response by $1/(0.6745 \sqrt{2D(2D+1)})$ [S7, unofficial].
-- **Haar Finite-Difference MAD**: Computes diagonal second differences $d = I(y,x) - I(y,x+1) - I(y+1,x) + I(y+1,x+1)$ and scales the median of $|d|$ by $1/(0.6745 \sqrt{2})$ [S7, unofficial].
-- **Formula**: $\text{Noise Floor PSNR} = 10 \log_{10}(1/\sigma^2)$ [S7, unofficial].
-- **Pitfall**: MAD estimators may return exactly zero for some volumes (e.g., Mouse heart nuclei), indicating the model is signal-limited, not noise-limited, and thus bounding nothing [S7, unofficial].
+This variant estimates the noise floor using Median Absolute Deviation (MAD) to determine the theoretical maximum PSNR (noise floor) for a given volume [S7, unofficial]. It uses estimators such as the Laplacian MAD or Haar finite-difference MAD to compute the noise standard deviation $\sigma$. The noise floor PSNR is then calculated as $10 \log_{10}(1/\sigma^2)$ [S7, unofficial]. A pitfall is that MAD estimators may return zero for signal-limited volumes (e.g., Mouse heart nuclei), indicating the model is not noise-limited [S7, unofficial].
 
 ### PSNR Loss
-PSNR Loss computes the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio between predicted and target images, used as a regression metric during training [S8, unofficial].
-- **Formula**: $\text{PSNR} = 20 \log_{10}\left(\frac{\text{max\_val}}{\sqrt{\text{MSE}}}\right)$ [S8, unofficial].
-- **Parameter**: `max_val` (default 1.0) is the maximum possible pixel value in the target image [S8, unofficial].
+PSNR Loss uses the PSNR formula as a regression metric during training [S8, unofficial]. It is computed as $\text{PSNR} = 20 \log_{10}(\text{max\_val} / \sqrt{\text{MSE}})$ [S8, unofficial]. While typically used as an evaluation metric, it can be adapted as a differentiable loss function, though MSE is more commonly used for optimization due to gradient stability [S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$, where $N$ is the total number of pixels/voxels. The calculation involves a single pass to compute squared differences and a mean operation.
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ if intermediate difference arrays are stored, or $O(1)$ if computed in a streaming fashion.
-- **Measured Performance**: For NeRF paper Blender scenes, a target PSNR is typically 31–33 dB [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Simple to compute, widely understood, and provides a single scalar value for comparison.
-- **Limitations**: Does not correlate well with human perception for high-frequency details or structural similarities. It is heavily biased towards large, smooth regions of the image [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use**: Quick baseline evaluation of photometric accuracy, especially when structural fidelity is less critical than intensity accuracy.
-- **When Not to Use**: When perceptual quality is the primary concern (use SSIM or LPIPS instead) or when evaluating geometric accuracy.
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- **Time:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the total number of pixels/voxels. The calculation requires a single pass to compute the squared differences and a constant-time logarithmic conversion.
+- **Memory:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space. Only scalar accumulators (sum of squared errors) are needed. The input arrays must be held in memory.
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+- On standard NeRF Blender scenes, target PSNR values are typically 31–33 dB [S2, unofficial].
+- A 3 dB increase represents a halving of the squared error, making PSNR sensitive to small improvements in high-quality reconstructions [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- **Strengths:** Fast to compute, easy to interpret, and widely used as a standard benchmark for comparing different rendering pipelines [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** It does not account for structural similarity or perceptual quality. It heavily weights large smooth areas and ignores small but visually important details (e.g., grass blades, specular highlights) [S1, unofficial]. It is also sensitive to pixel misalignment.
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use PSNR for quick, objective comparison of photometric accuracy during training logging or when comparing methods on standard benchmarks [S2, unofficial].
+- Do not rely on PSNR alone for final quality assessment; combine with SSIM, LPIPS, or human evaluation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-PSNR originated as a standard metric in image and video processing to evaluate compression algorithms and reconstruction quality [S4]. It has been widely adopted in the field of novel view synthesis and radiance field optimization (e.g., NeRF, 3D Gaussian Splatting) as a primary benchmark for photometric fidelity [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. The metric is often reported alongside SSIM and LPIPS to provide a more comprehensive evaluation of reconstruction quality [S1, unofficial].
+PSNR originated in signal processing and image compression as a measure of reconstruction quality [S4]. In the context of volume rendering and NeRFs, it has become a standard metric for evaluating novel view synthesis quality [S2, unofficial]. The metric is defined in foundational papers such as those evaluating Deep Learning Reconstruction methods [S4] and is widely cited in recent surveys of 3D Gaussian Splatting pipelines [S1, unofficial].
 
